The `❌ Runtime stream intelligence { error: 'runtime is not defined' }` error is caused by a **JavaScript scoping mistake** in the final part of your test script.

### The problem
At the end of `tests/deepseek-full-suite.mjs`, you have:

```javascript
// ... after browser.close() ...

try {
  try {
    let runtime = null;          // runtime is block-scoped here
    try {
      if (!page.isClosed()) {
        runtime = await runtimeIntel.exportRuntime(page);
      }
    } catch (e) { ... }

    // ... save runtime file ...
  } catch (e) { ... }

  // ⚠️ runtime is NOT defined here – it was only inside the inner try block
  runtime = runtime || { streams: [], fetches: [], ... };
  console.log("✅ Runtime stream intelligence");
  // ...
} catch (e) {
  console.log("❌ Runtime stream intelligence");
  console.log({ error: e.message });  // "runtime is not defined"
}
```

Because `let runtime = null` is declared **inside** the inner `try` block, it is not visible in the outer block after the inner `try` finishes. The line `runtime = runtime || { ... }` throws a `ReferenceError`, which is caught by the outer `catch`, printing the error you see.

### How to fix it
Move the `runtime` declaration **outside** the inner `try` so it’s accessible later:

```javascript
let runtime = null;   // <-- declare here, before any try blocks

try {
  try {
    if (!page.isClosed()) {
      runtime = await runtimeIntel.exportRuntime(page);
    }
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('❌ Runtime export failed');
    console.error(e);
  }

  const runtimePath = path.join(REPORT_DIR, `runtime-${Date.now()}.json`);
  runtime = runtime || { streams: [], fetches: [], events: [], recovered: true };
  fs.writeFileSync(runtimePath, JSON.stringify(runtime, null, 2));
  console.log("✅ Runtime intelligence export");
  console.log({ runtimePath });

} catch (e) {
  console.error("❌ Runtime export failed");
  console.error(e);
}

// Now runtime is still defined here
runtime = runtime || { streams: [], fetches: [], wsMessages: [], domMutations: [], lastStream: null };
console.log("✅ Runtime stream intelligence");
console.log(JSON.stringify({
  fetches: runtime?.fetches.length,
  streams: (runtime?.streams || []).length,
  completions: (runtime?.completions || []).length,
  uploads: (runtime?.uploads || []).length
}, null, 2));
```

Optionally, you can simplify by removing the outer `try-catch` that only handled this error – with the fix, you won't get a `ReferenceError` there, but the inner `try-catch` for the export itself can stay.

After this change, the runtime intelligence will be reported correctly, and the error will disappear.

